WEBVTT

00:00:09.239 --> 00:00:16.982 
In the previous tutorial, we explored Interaction Affordances — properties, actions, and events — and how they describe what a

00:00:16.982 --> 00:00:18.024 
Thing can do.

00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:25.240 
In this video, we will focus on the next important question: How do those interactions actually happen over the network?

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:32.853 
 In the Web of Things, this is handled through bindings. Bindings define how a Consumer communicates with a Thing

00:00:32.853 --> 00:00:42.919 
using concrete protocols like HTTP, CoAP, MQTT, and others, or how the data is serialized, such as JSON, text, or

00:00:42.919 --> 00:00:43.753 
CBOR.

00:00:43.753 --> 00:00:49.941 
 A binding maps an operation of an interaction affordance — such as reading a property or invoking an action

00:00:49.941 --> 00:00:57.955 
— to a specific network message which includes the communication protocol and endpoint, and the parameters required by the protocol.

00:00:57.955 --> 00:01:04.229 
By the end of this video, you'll understand how the Consumer knows where to send a request, which protocol to

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:06.755 
use, and how to encode the data.

00:01:06.755 --> 00:01:13.548 
 A form describes a way to interact with an affordance over a specific protocol. It can be thought of

00:01:13.548 --> 00:01:20.503 
as a simple instruction to the Consumer: "To perform this type of operation, on this affordance, send a request, in

00:01:20.503 --> 00:01:25.331 
this way, to this address." Now let's break down the key parts of a form.

00:01:25.331 --> 00:01:30.133 
Each form can declare one or more operations, using the "op" field.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:38.934 
Operations describe what semantic actions the Consumer can perform — for example: reading or writing a property, invoking an action,

00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:40.634 
or subscribing to an event.

00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:46.949 
These operation types are defined by the WoT specification and are independent of any specific protocol.

00:01:46.949 --> 00:01:50.349 
You can find a full list on the TD specification.

00:01:50.349 --> 00:01:56.002 
If the "op" field is omitted, default operations are inferred based on the affordance type.

00:01:56.002 --> 00:02:03.409 
For example, forms of a readable property are assumed to include the "readproperty" operation unless stated otherwise.

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:07.859 
 The most important field in a form is " href".

00:02:07.859 --> 00:02:14.331 
The "href" is a URI that tells the client where to interact with the Thing and which protocol to use.

00:02:14.331 --> 00:02:18.393 
The protocol is inferred directly from the URI scheme.

00:02:18.393 --> 00:02:26.572 
 This design allows the Thing Description concept to stay protocol-agnostic while still enabling concrete protocol-level interactions.

00:02:26.572 --> 00:02:33.209 
A single affordance can expose multiple forms as well, offering the same interaction over different protocols.

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:39.579 
 Forms also specify a "contentType", which tells the client how the payload is encoded.

00:02:39.579 --> 00:02:44.115 
Common examples include JSON, CBOR and plain text formats.

00:02:44.115 --> 00:02:49.859 
 This ensures that both the Thing and the Consumer agree on how data is serialized and parsed.

00:02:49.859 --> 00:02:57.971 
If no content type is specified, protocol-specific defaults may apply, but explicitly declaring them improves interoperability.

00:02:57.971 --> 00:03:07.647 
 While WoT aims to stay protocol-independent, forms allow protocol-specific extensions when needed. These are expressed through additional fields defined

00:03:07.647 --> 00:03:10.203 
in protocol binding specifications.

00:03:10.203 --> 00:03:17.462 
 Now that we understand the structure of forms, let's see how protocol bindings work in practice.

00:03:17.462 --> 00:03:23.509 
When you want to write a Consumer application, you don't need any prior knowledge about the protocol being used.

00:03:23.509 --> 00:03:31.078 
Instead, the Consumer reads the Thing Description, looks at the available forms, and selects one it understands.

00:03:31.078 --> 00:03:36.061 
 Let's walk through four protocols using our smart coffee machine example.

00:03:36.061 --> 00:03:41.888 
For each one, we'll trace exactly how a WoT operation relates to real network traffic.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:51.994 
 First, we want the Consumer to perform the " readProperty" operation on "coffeeBeansLeft" so we can get the remaining

00:03:51.994 --> 00:03:57.600 
 To achieve this, the Consumer reads the form and translates it into a network request.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.475 
The " href" tells it the address and that HTTP is the protocol.

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:10.132 
Then the "htv:methodName" field says to use a " GET" request and the "contentType" tells it to expect a JSON

00:04:10.132 --> 00:04:11.042 
in return.

00:04:11.042 --> 00:04:13.820 
So the Consumer sends the following request:

